# Summary
Bug introduced in this commit: parse_amz_date lacks safe ASCII validation while the time-skew check calls it on requests.
parse_amz_date assumes ASCII and slices the timestamp at fixed byte offsets (e.g., ts[0..4], ts[4..6]). For a 16‑byte UTF‑8 string containing multibyte characters that do not align with those offsets, Rust will panic due to invalid UTF‑8 boundaries. Because authenticate() calls parse_amz_date on every signed request, an authenticated client can send a crafted x-amz-date header that triggers a panic and terminates the server process, resulting in a denial of service.

# Reviewer note
This report was valid for the historical `14d95e1` implementation, but it is already fixed in the current tree.

The fix landed in commit `05c8412` (`auth: start request parser hardening`), which replaced the raw `str[..]` timestamp slicing with byte-oriented ASCII parsing in `parse_amz_date` and added regression coverage for malformed multibyte input.

