# Attack-path analysis
Final: low | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: medium | Policy adjusted: medium
## Rationale
Although the bug is a real panic reachable via the HTTP authentication path, it requires valid SigV4 credentials and only impacts availability. The project is single-tenant by design and defaults to localhost listening, reducing realistic attacker reach. Therefore, the risk is lowered from medium to low.
## Likelihood
high - Exploitation requires valid credentials and a crafted UTF-8 header; default listening on localhost reduces exposure unless reconfigured. | Remote network vector
## Impact
medium - A panic in the request authentication path terminates the single-process HTTP server, causing service-wide unavailability until restart.
## Assumptions
- The server is reachable over the network (ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR not restricted to localhost in deployment).
- An attacker can obtain valid SigV4 credentials (ARGMIN_ACCESS_KEY_ID/ARGMIN_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY) or abuse leaked credentials.
- tiny_http accepts UTF-8 header values containing multibyte characters without rejecting them.
- Valid SigV4 credentials to pass verify_request
- Ability to send a signed request with a 16-byte UTF-8 x-amz-date header containing multibyte characters
## Path
attacker -> HTTP endpoint -> authenticate(parse_amz_date) -> panic -> server down
## Path evidence
- `crates/auth/src/canonical.rs:180-192` - parse_amz_date slices fixed byte offsets on a UTF-8 string without ASCII validation, which can panic on multibyte boundaries.
- `crates/server/src/http/mod.rs:121-156` - authenticate() calls parse_amz_date on x-amz-date for every signed request.
- `crates/server/src/config.rs:19-34` - Default listen address is 127.0.0.1:9000, configurable via ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR.
## Narrative
The HTTP frontend authenticates every request and enforces time-skew using parse_amz_date on the x-amz-date header. parse_amz_date slices the header string at fixed byte offsets without ensuring ASCII boundaries, which can panic for valid UTF-8 strings with multibyte characters. Because authenticate() runs for every signed request, an attacker with valid credentials can craft a 16-byte UTF-8 x-amz-date to trigger a panic and terminate the server process, causing a denial of service.
## Controls
- SigV4 authentication required (Authorization header)
- Request body size limits enforced in request parsing
## Blindspots
- No deployment manifests found to confirm actual network exposure.
- tiny_http header validation behavior not verified at runtime in this analysis.
- No dynamic test of the full server request path due to offline build constraints.
